# Planning in India: From Five-Year Plans to NITI Aayog

> For sixty-six years India decided, in five-year instalments, how
> much to invest and where. From the First Plan's dams and fields to
> NITI Aayog's dashboards and indices, this lesson traces how Indian
> planning was built, tested, reformed, and finally reimagined.

## 1. Why Plan? The Post-Independence Vision

[definition]
**Economic planning** is the deliberate, central direction of an
economy's investment and production priorities by the state for a
stated period, usually through quantified targets for output,
savings, and sectoral growth.
[/definition]

After 1947, planners saw private capital as too thin and too
suspicious of long-gestation investments to build steel plants,
dams, and machine industries quickly. Influenced by the Soviet model
and by thinkers such as P. C. Mahalanobis, India chose a **mixed
economy**: the state would occupy the commanding heights while
private enterprise continued in consumer goods.

The **Planning Commission** was set up by a Government of India
cabinet resolution on **15 March 1950** — not by statute — with the
Prime Minister as its chair (Jawaharlal Nehru was the first). Its job
was to assess resources, formulate plans, and advise on priorities;
execution stayed with ministries and states. The National Development
Council, created in **1952**, brought Chief Ministers into plan
approval.

## 2. The Machinery of Planning

@diagram tree id="planning-machinery" layout="tree" aria="Institutions of Indian planning"
  "Indian planning" -> "Planning Commission (1950-2014)"
  "Indian planning" -> "Five-Year Plans (1951-2017)"
  "Planning Commission (1950-2014)" -> "Chair: Prime Minister"
  "Planning Commission (1950-2014)" -> "Deputy Chairman and members"
  "Five-Year Plans (1951-2017)" -> "Approved by National Development Council (1952)"
  "Five-Year Plans (1951-2017)" -> "Annual plans in gap years"
@/diagram

Each plan set a growth target, allocated gross budgetary support
among ministries, and divided resources between the Centre and the
states. A plan year ran from April to March, and a plan "period"
spanned five financial years — so the First Plan is written
**1951-56**, not 1951-55.

## 3. The First Three Plans: Foundations and Shocks (1951-1966)

| Plan | Period | Model / focus | Target | Achieved (about) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| First | 1951-56 | Harrod-Domar; agriculture, irrigation, power | 2.1% | 3.6% |
| Second | 1956-61 | Mahalanobis; heavy industry | 4.5% | 4.3% |
| Third | 1961-66 | Self-sustaining growth; "agriculture and industry" | 5.6% | 2.4% |

The **First Plan** succeeded so well that its problem was surfeit:
good monsoons and community development lifted food output and
tamed the inflation left by Partition and war.

The **Second Plan** was the Mahalanobis era: the Industrial Policy
Resolution of **1956** classified industries into Schedule A (state
monopoly — railways, mining, steel), Schedule B (state-led), and
Schedule C (private). Steel plants rose at Bhilai (Soviet aid),
Rourkela (West German), and Durgapur (British).

The **Third Plan** was wrecked by shocks no model had budgeted for:
the **1962 China war** and the **1965 Pakistan war** inflated defence
spending while two consecutive **droughts (1965, 1966)** cut food
grain output sharply. The rupee was devalued by about **36.5 per
cent in June 1966**, and the plan lapsed into **three Annual Plans
(1966-69)** — the first "plan holiday".

## 4. Plan Holidays, Garibi Hatao, and the Middle Years (1966-1990)

Crisis bred doctrine. With food imports near famine levels, the
**Green Revolution** spread high-yielding seeds, fertiliser, and
assured irrigation through Punjab, Haryana, and western Uttar Pradesh
from 1966-67, and India's food-grain output climbed from about
72 million tonnes (1965-66) past 100 million tonnes by 1976-77.

| Plan | Period | Slogan / theme | Achieved growth (about) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Fourth | 1969-74 | "Growth with stability" | 3.3% |
| Fifth | 1974-79 | Poverty removal and self-reliance ("Garibi Hatao" backdrop) | 4.8% |
| — | 1978-80 | Rolling Plan concept under the Janata government | — |
| Sixth | 1980-85 | Direct attack on poverty: IRDP, NREP | 5.7% |
| Seventh | 1985-90 | Productivity; food, work | 6.0% |

The **Fifth Plan** was cut short in 1978 when the Janata government
replaced it with the **Rolling Plan** — a plan revised every year.
The **Sixth Plan** institutionalised anti-poverty programmes: the
Integrated Rural Development Programme (IRDP) and the National Rural
Employment Programme (NREP), ancestors of today's MGNREGA. The
**1980-92** stretch also saw two more plan interruptions (annual
plans in 1979-80 and 1990-92), the latter caused by the fiscal and
external instability that ended in the 1991 crisis.

[warning]
Do not confuse the two "holidays": **1966-69** followed the Third
Plan (wars and drought); **1990-92** followed the Seventh (political
instability and the balance-of-payments slide). Rolling Plan =
1978-80, a Janata idea, not a holiday as such.
[/warning]

## 5. The Reform-Era Plans (1992-2017)

After liberalisation, plans shifted from command to indicative
planning — from controlling licences to financing infrastructure and
social sectors.

| Plan | Period | Theme | Achieved growth (about) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Eighth | 1992-97 | Reforms and growth | 6.8% |
| Ninth | 1997-2002 | "Growth with social justice" | 5.4% |
| Tenth | 2002-07 | Monitoring targets | 7.7% |
| Eleventh | 2007-12 | "Faster and more inclusive growth" | 8.0% |
| Twelfth | 2012-17 | "Faster, more inclusive and sustainable growth" | about 6.8% |

