The trend line tells the story: growth roughly **doubled** from the
Nehru-Mahalanobis years to the reform era, with the Eleventh Plan's
~8 per cent marking the pre-crisis peak. The **Twelfth Plan
(2012-17)** was the last of the line; its term ended before a
successor plan could be drafted.

## 6. What Planning Achieved — and Where It Failed

Achievements, by any fair audit, are substantial:

- **Food security**: food-grain output rose from about 55 million
  tonnes in 1950-51 to over 300 million tonnes in recent years — the
  Green Revolution plus procurement and the PDS.
- **An industrial and scientific base**: PSUs in steel, machine
  tools, power (BHEL), atomic energy and space; the IITs and CSIR
  labs; dams under the Bhakra-Nangal and similar multipurpose
  projects.
- **Savings and investment** rose from about 10 per cent of GDP in
  the 1950s to well above 30 per cent before the crisis years.

Criticisms are equally standard exam fare:

- The **licence-permit-quota raj** throttled competition: industrial
  licensing, import controls, and price controls bred scarcity,
  queues, and rent-seeking.
- **PSU inefficiency**: many public enterprises earned returns below
  the cost of capital, draining the budget.
- **Plan growth was capital-heavy and job-light**, so unemployment
  and inequality persisted despite rising output.
- **Federal friction**: plan allocations were seen as partisan, and
  states complained of one-size-fits-all schemes.

## 7. From Planning Commission to NITI Aayog

On 15 August 2014 the Prime Minister announced the Planning
Commission's replacement from the Red Fort; a cabinet resolution of
**1 January 2015** created the **National Institution for
Transforming India — NITI Aayog**.

[important]
Key structural differences: NITI Aayog is a **think tank and
coordinating body, not an allocator** — it distributes no plan funds,
since plan transfers were replaced by the Finance Commission's tax
devolution formula (14 per cent, later 41 per cent, of the divisible
pool). It has no power to enforce; its leverage is evidence, indices,
and persuasion.
[/important]

| Feature | Planning Commission | NITI Aayog |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Set up | 15 March 1950 | 1 January 2015 |
| Role | Allocate funds, approve plans | Policy ideas, monitoring, coordination |
| Federal design | Top-down; states consulted via NDC | "Cooperative and competitive federalism"; Governing Council of all CMs |
| Signature documents | Five-Year Plans, Annual Plans | 15-year Vision, 7-year Strategy, 3-year Action Agenda |

The **Governing Council** — Prime Minister, Chief Ministers, and
Lt-Governor representatives — meets to set the national agenda, and
regional councils address area-specific issues. The first CEO was
Amitabh Kant.

## 8. NITI Aayog's Tools: Federalism by Dashboard

NITI Aayog governs by measurement. Its flagship instruments:

- **SDG India Index** — ranks states and UTs on progress toward the
  2030 Sustainable Development Goals; the national score rose from
  57 in the 2018 baseline to 71 in the 2023-24 edition, with Kerala
  topping every edition.
- **Aspirational Districts Programme (2018)** — 112 of the least
  developed districts monitored monthly on health, nutrition,
  education, and basic infrastructure.
- **Multidimensional Poverty Index (National MPI)** — India's own 12
  indicators of deprivation: poverty fell from about **24.85 per
  cent (2015-16) to 14.96 per cent (2019-21)**, moving roughly 13.5
  crore people out of multidimensional poverty.
- **Composite Water Management Index, Innovation Index, and health
  and school "health-card" reports** — competitive pressure by
  publication.

[tip]
Memory hook: Planning Commission = *funds*; NITI Aayog = *facts*.
1950 vs 2015; allocation vs acceleration; NDC approval vs Governing
Council persuasion.
[/tip]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Item | Fact |
| --- | --- |
| Planning Commission | Cabinet resolution, 15 March 1950; PM chairs |
| First Plan | 1951-56, Harrod-Domar model, agriculture priority |
| Second Plan | 1956-61, Mahalanobis model, heavy industry |
| First plan holiday | 1966-69, after wars and droughts |
| Green Revolution begins | 1966-67, HYV seeds in Punjab-Haryana-west UP |
| Rolling Plan | 1978-80, Janata government |
| Poverty slogan era | Fifth Plan, "Garibi Hatao" backdrop |
| Reform-era plans | Eighth (1992-97) onward; Twelfth (2012-17) last |
| NITI Aayog | 1 January 2015; think tank, no fund allocation |
| NITI indices | SDG India Index, Aspirational Districts, National MPI |
